{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs","id":1343519,"title":"Social rolls against PCs","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-10-24T17:24:45-07:00","id":1343519,"title":"Social rolls against PCs","content":"Hi. I'd like to know how you guys deal with social skills against PCs. Of course someone (PC or NPC) that uses Mind 4 to possess a PC or a vampire that uses Dominate, is going to take control of said PC victim actions. But what if instead of directly taking control of their actions, a more subtle approach is taken. Do you allow social skill rolls like manipulation+subterfuge or any other roll (I think intimidation is allowed in almost all tables) that change the behavior of the PC and how you do it?<br /> <br /> I've seen people say that we should never mess with a PC &quot;free will&quot;, but as I see, just like a Dominated PC, a persuaded one should follow the character and let himself be persuaded in the case of a successful rol against him.<br /> <br /> For the ones that answers negatively (as in, doesn't allow persuasion rolls against PC), what if a char uses magick to influence the outcome of the persuasion? For example a Mind mage that alters the PC's mood so he is more prone to accept suggestions. Or a Life mage the mess with hormones or, even better, simulates drugs that takes part of the free will of a person (scopolamine or some kind of anesthesia). Normally that would affect the rolls of the skill modifying the diff. But if you don't allow rolls against PC, how do you rule, for example, a PC using Life to drug another PC to try and manipulate him??","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-24T18:56:10-07:00","id":1343524,"title":"","content":"I allow NPCs abd PCs to make social rolls against PCs, though the WoD mechanics are lacking a bit in some of the more recent design approaches to help make this go over more smoothly.<br /> <br /> If I'm playing with people a bit resistant to this, I use the following:<br /> <br /> If you go along with a social roll following the letter and spirit of the statement, regain a WP point.<br /> Following the spirit of the social roll to the best of your character's ability, including circumstances changing to make it reasonable to not follow (&quot;hey can you sneak this into the club for me&quot; when there's no obvious security, but then three guards step out to watch the door), has no benefit or cost.<br /> Following the letter of the roll but knowingly going against the spirit of it, or trying to subvert it in a significant fashion costs a WP point.<br /> <br /> It helps ensure mundane social rolls aren't viewed as mind-control, but there's incentive to go with the flow.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-25T05:48:58-07:00","id":1343564,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> I see. That is a good rule, but I think the player will always try to find an excuse to try to fit into the no benefit, no cost situation. <br /> <br /> And what if the PC was magickally druged to obey (Life 3 or Mind 2)? <br /> Also, what if the request is something that won't put the PC in risk, but it is something he would never do, sober at least, like imitating a chicken?<br /> <br /> PS: M20 is quite confusing. In rev we had fast-talk to convince ppl. What would be the roll in M20 for that? Is it resisted?<br /> I see that lying is deception, which is resisted against WP probably. But persuasion is not in the list..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3153,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","user":"Lord Revan","date":". 10-25-2019, 08:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-25T10:04:42-07:00","id":1343605,"title":"","content":"This is something we ought to break down into several different categories, in order to keep wires from getting crossed and statements that apply to one thing not be applied to others. The way I see it, there are three distinct &quot;levels&quot; of manipulation that may be applied by NPC's to PC's.<br /> <br /> The first being the simplest, straight up controlling effects -- Mind, certain applications of Life, extending into other game systems Dominate, etc. These represent direct loss of character control, and thus player agency, and are almost always going to stem from supernatural abilities. Honestly, these are going to be the easiest to adjudicate; due to the loss of control over their character however temporarily, and generally handled in the most straightforward manner. No player likes losing that degree of agency over their character and won't be happy about it, so it's my personal policy to not piss on the player's head and tell them it's raining, but on the other hand this is the most sparingly-used layer.<br /> <br /> The second being more aggressive -- threats of violence or other consequences, browbeating or bullying, peer pressure, and the like. This would be typically mundane, but supernatural abilities that place characters in a certain state of mind would count as well. These actions compel characters or players to act in a certain way, but not necessarily forced in the sense the player loses complete agency. Here, it's best to use the old carrot and stick so the player complies, but of their own volition -- experience points for roleplaying manipulation well, mandatory Willpower expenditure to resist, or the looming threat of whatever consequences for resistance -- and certainly free to choose <i>how</i> they may comply.<br /> <br /> The third being almost-always mundane, but may be <i>aided</i> by supernatural abilities (such as using Mind to reduce the difficulty of a roll), efforts where the player retains full agency. This is where bargaining, appeals to mutual or self interest, and most deception attempts lie. Here, it's a &quot;take it or leave it&quot; scenario where the player decides for themselves how to react, and in these situations it's best to only incentivize a given course of action.<br /> <br /> Obviously, there's a spectrum here between supernatural and mundane actions, with less agency to the player the &quot;more&quot; supernatural the attempt to manipulate them. Some mundane things, like the influence of psychoactive drugs, being the exception, but realistically they follow the same scheme and spectrum and really don't need much discussion to their own. And of course, degrees of success and external considerations matter as well -- basic success on a supernatural controlling effect may give the player a bit of leeway, but dramatic success on a totally mundane persuasion attempt may push matters into the second, rather than third, category.<br /> <br /> The only major odd man out are attempts to deceive PC's, which can get really dirty, really fast because there's always that X-factor that, on no matter how good a roll the ST makes for the NPC, there'll always be some inconsistency, factoid, tell, or leap of logic players can draw upon to say &quot;well it sounds good, but my character doesn't completely buy it&quot;. To really get to the heart of that statement, let's do a quick character study: Walter White from Breaking Bad.<br /> <br /> Walt's hands-down the shittiest liar on the show, but he's the best manipulator. I don't get how or why the show's viewers and fans have it in their head he's this amazing, world-class liar; he oversells and draws attention to his lies, and the best he's capable of is providing those who want to believe him an excuse to not think too hard about it. Which is the key point, whether targets are predisposed to believing the liar, and in terms of game mechanics that's not something directly controllable by a storyteller. Where Walt excels is weaponizing truth and the flaws of those around him.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-25T11:49:23-07:00","id":1343619,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1343564#post1343564\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That is a good rule, but I think the player will always try to find an excuse to try to fit into the no benefit, no cost situation.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not been my experience, but it's not really that bad of a result, since it means they're honoring the successful social roll against them, and in a way that affirms player agency instead of dismissing it (even if funneling them into a forced choice, but all player agency is constrained by forced choices on some level).<br /> <br /> The WoD mundane social rules (as inconsistent as they are over multiple editions and multiple game lines) have a general commonality of &quot;resolve the roll(s), and spend WP to ignore the results if you don't like being the loser because it's not mind-control.&quot; As such, players tend to feel like ignoring having their characters being good at resisting the initial roll is less important than having that WP, since that WP is the only way to exert agency despite it just being mundane social resolution.<br /> <br /> The three tiered approach gives players more options. Given how valuable WP can be for mages in high stress situations, taking a free WP is usually good incentive to entice some level of accepting successful social rolls against them. As well, the &quot;subvert&quot; rather than, &quot;ignore&quot; language is important. Subverting the social roll made against you means you're still engaging with it, rather than the RAW encouraging you to just shut social rolls down completely.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And what if the PC was magickally druged to obey (Life 3 or Mind 2)? </div> </div> </div>As neither of these are direct mind control, I would simply apply the Magic Enhancing Abilities rules, and reduce the difficulty of mundane social rolls used after the PC is the target of such influence.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, what if the request is something that won't put the PC in risk, but it is something he would never do, sober at least, like imitating a chicken? </div> </div> </div>House rule 2: Have a standard table of difficulty modifiers to mundane social rolls, layered on top of the default difficulties (also consider modifying the default difficulties based on what the roll is actually about). Include standard traits (Nature/Demeanor, Essence, Resonance if using the Revised/M20 optional rules for that), faction philosophy, familiarity, and social preconceptions as all things that can be a positive or negative modifier based on situation and approach. A Technocrat is going to have an easier time bluffing about threatening a Tradition mage with scrutiny or worse, than a Technocrat is going to have convincing a Tradition mage that they mean no harm and have something of mutual benefit to offer.<br /> <br /> This easily adjudicates in a more predictable fashion - and thus lets players build characters appropriately towards their intent - but never really makes you a social wall people can't get to; they just need to approach the right way (or use supernatural powers to smooth over the difference). If someone comes at your PC with a social attempt that ends up a diff 13 or 14, either it's going auto-fail or get bumped to the threshold rules... and good luck pulling that off. Even with magic helping (opposed to overt mind control) it's still something that requires some finesse instead of powering through.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-25T14:24:23-07:00","id":1343657,"title":"","content":"While the best for mundane persuasion is indeed to tell the player that he must follow the order (afterall, his character was convinced), and offer the expenditure of WP to resist, I don't think that's the case when dealing with magickally enhanced rolls.<br /> <br /> Usimg Mind or Life, you can simulate the effect that drugs have in your brain. There is a drug called scopolamine (it appears in the series &quot;The OA&quot;) that completely turn the user into a puppet. There are cases of people giving everything they had to &quot;robers&quot;, &quot;willingly&quot;, and not remembering well afterwards. Other drugs, like anesthesia can make you a complete fool, as easily to trick as a child.<br /> I think those should had stronger mechanics than just mldifiers. A modifier is something natural, like &quot;you like the person who's making the suggestion then you she gets -1 diff ln her roll&quot;. This is shallow and mundane.<br /> <br /> Maybe if we remove the option of WP exenditure for magickally enhanced persuasion?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-25T15:02:07-07:00","id":1343663,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1343657#post1343657\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...I don't think that's the case when dealing with magickally enhanced rolls.</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends on magic. Magically getting someone drunk should have the same impact as getting them normally drunk, it's just a different method. Saying that magical drunkenness should be different than normal drunkenness is saying that you're doing more than getting them drunk.<br /> <br /> If you want to magically control people, just use the magic that does that and you can get around mundane social rolls (even magically boosted ones).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There is a drug called scopolamine (it appears in the series &quot;The OA&quot;) that completely turn the user into a puppet. </div> </div> </div>It does no such thing. There are no real drugs that accomplish this.<br /> <br /> Scopolamine can result in memory loss, and reduced cognitive function.. but that's not during someone into a puppet.<br /> <br /> Like all attempts at mind control drugs, or truth serums, it doesn't work, because our brains are too complicated for these sorts of things. As well, dosing strangers that you don't know the medical history of? You're going to kill them far more often than you're going to chemically induce a state useful for socially influencing them.<br /> <br /> You might be able to use such drugs regarding Mythic Threads or Blatancy to help hide what you're doing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think those should had stronger mechanics than just mldifiers. </div> </div> </div>That's what Mind 4 is for.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-25T21:05:54-07:00","id":1343712,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1343619#post1343619\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The three tiered approach gives players more options. Given how valuable WP can be for mages in high stress situations, taking a free WP is usually good incentive to entice some level of accepting successful social rolls against them. As well, the &quot;subvert&quot; rather than, &quot;ignore&quot; language is important. Subverting the social roll made against you means you're still engaging with it, rather than the RAW encouraging you to just shut social rolls down completely.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is also an issue where things really do have to be taken on an <i>ad hoc</i> basis, in accordance with the character -- their character backstory, Nature, permanent Willpower, and relevant merits and flaws -- and the circumstances at hand. As I said, I prefer to award experience for good role play, and issues with Willpower restoration I leave to whether or not the character is acting in accordance (or against) their Nature. For instance, there is no justifiable reason to award a Willpower point to a character with a Soldier nature, if they're acting to subvert valid orders given to them by a clear superior that happen to be against that character's interest. In fact, this is a case where a Willpower expenditure may be requested, where that may not be the case for others, especially for opposed archetypes like Rogue where the carrot and stick may be reversed.<br /> <br /> The gold standard should be whether or not the player is acting in character, not necessarily what they're doing in a vacuum.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-26T00:23:35-07:00","id":1343728,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1343712#post1343712\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is also an issue where things really do have to be taken on an <i>ad hoc</i> basis, in accordance with the character -- their character backstory, Nature, permanent Willpower, and relevant merits and flaws -- and the circumstances at hand.</div> </div> </div> </div>I disagree it has to be ad hoc; see having a table of modifiers.<br /> <br /> Ad hoc leads to arguments where people think they can be immune to mundane social influence no matter how they build their characters because they'll latch on to one thing of that list and insist that nothing anyone could say without powers could possibly work.<br /> <br /> Making it clear how these elements impact a character's ability to resist social influence sets the appropriate expectations that lets players know how to build characters resistant to social influence in general, as well as more and less resistant situationally.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As I said, I prefer to award experience for good role play,... </div> </div> </div>There's already an XP reward in the books for good role-play. I don't see any good for increasing the reward just for this circumstance, and there needs to be an incentive for more than one social roll per session.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...and issues with Willpower restoration I leave to whether or not the character is acting in accordance (or against) their Nature. </div> </div> </div>M20 already has more ways to gain WP than Nature, and yes, it's a house rule. But WP is pretty much the only resource in the game that's appropriate incentive (can apply multiple times in a session, is valuable enough to encourage players, isn't so valuable players aren't going to feel too put out if they don't take it).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For instance, there is no justifiable reason to award a Willpower point to a character with a Soldier nature, if they're acting to subvert valid orders given to them by a clear superior that happen to be against that character's interest. </div> </div> </div>In my house rules, subverting an order backed by a successful social roll (because it's against the characters interest and thus worth rolling in the first place), would cost WP, not reward it. At best it might neither reward or cost if the order causes a detrimental enough that the Soldier would take it as an illegal or unjust order.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-26T08:00:26-07:00","id":1343765,"title":"","content":"Ways you can use the social stats and rolls of NPCs, on PCs:<br /> <br /> - make it more about playing to the crowd; if the NPC gets 5 successes on an Expression roll to convince the PC to follow their plan, and the PC says no and demands they use their plan, then bystander NPCs (and perhaps PCs who feel 100% happy playing along) will call out the PC for rejecting such a brilliant plan. This applies to lots of situations - you're ALLOWED to storm out when your senior, a very commanding mage, tells you that you have to stay. His 5 successes don't force you to stay. But if you leave, those who observe this will think much more poorly of you.<br /> <br /> - place emphasis on the PCs keeping autonomy, but losing some of their effectiveness. Intimidation doesn't force you to flee, but it might give you a -2 dice pool modifier.<br /> <br /> - place emphasis on what the PC observes, rather than how they feel. Lying is a social roll. The player absolutely knows this NPC is lying, but you have to frame it as &quot;they seem to be telling the truth, very convincingly&quot; in an in-character sense. Because otherwise there's no point to the NPC rolling Manipulation + Subterfuge and the PC rolling Perception + Empathy. And then you can expect the PC to play it out as if what they were told was true, OR as if the NPC who lied to them believed what they were saying was true.<br /> <br /> - Same with pity, or flirtation, or being charmed... don't tell the PC that they feel pity, or that they're turned on, or that they feel charmed. Tell them what they observe, and perhaps highlight how an &quot;average&quot; person might respond. Lots of my players will respond with stuff like &quot;oh yeah my gal is an idiot, she'd fall for that&quot; or &quot;well my guy doesn't have much experience with beautiful women and he'd probably be taken in by this, at least for now&quot;. On the other hand they might say &quot;my character was raised by narcissists and wouldn't buy into a pity parade - she's seen this guy being a piece of shit, and she isn't buying what he's selling&quot; or &quot;the flirting is impressive, but they're REALLY barking up the wrong tree since my PC is utterly oblivious&quot;.<br /> <br /> So I think the bottom line is that it's easy to get this right with the right table dynamic, and a focus on how the character would respond. &quot;How would you respond in character&quot; breaks down ENTIRELY with bad players though, since their character always has to be right and always has to win. Thankfully I've only experienced that once and I have nothing like that at my current table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 10-26-2019, 11:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-26T08:36:39-07:00","id":1343779,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1343728#post1343728\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ad hoc leads to arguments where people think they can be immune to mundane social influence no matter how they build their characters because they'll latch on to one thing of that list and insist that nothing anyone could say without powers could possibly work.</div> </div> </div> </div>Once again, that's a matter of what level of influence is being applied on a per-case basis; this is why I made it clear it's important to consider the source of the influence, the means employed to effect it, and the totality of circumstances around the roll. Not all social rolls <i>are</i> equal or exist in a vacuum, and a key part of this conversation is that not all social rolls are or should be considered &quot;my way or the highway&quot; affairs; that was my earlier point about how modes of operation lie on a spectrum between level of influence, player agency in the wake of that influence, and consequent awards or penalties. An intimidation check <i>shouldn't</i> have equal weight as an attempt to lie, persuade, or negotiate a deal -- different means of exerting influence should have different outcomes in the wake of a player decision to comply, subvert, or resist.<br /> <br /> Likewise, these consequences may not, and shouldn't be, always immediate or obvious, but rather manifest themselves in the medium and short term as they determine character relationships over time in the course of an ongoing narrative. In other words, this is not a situation appropriately handled within the sole, or even primary, purview of crunch.<br /> <br /> The situation of one or more players feeling like they can assert their way out of NPC social influence at all times and under any circumstances is indicative of a player or storyteller problem, not a game mechanics problem. Players can and certainly should be free to work with <i>most</i> attempts at influence -- in sum, not merely mundane or supernatural as the majority of influence checks that happen over the course of a chronicle should be mundane -- especially considering players' attempts to work around influence more often than not leads to interesting plot developments that may otherwise not occur. However, when it comes to otherwise critical moments, players should understand dicking around <i>will</i> cause blowback.<br /> <br /> Likewise, it is incumbent upon storytellers to not always handle circumstances the same, be subversive in turn, and handle situations in ways that make players feel subverting or resisting attempts to influence them is not always the best course of action. That antagonist NPC won't underestimate the PC's next time around, or maybe compliance in the short term will lead to later strategic errors or underestimation; maybe the Master that warns PC's off from a certain part of town is aware of a threat the PC's aren't equipped to handle; maybe that NPC Ally is lying about missing a meeting to protect the PC from knowledge or entanglements they were better off without; maybe accepting a poor deal now leads to better long-term returns thanks to an improved professional relationship.<br /> <br /> The point being, if the players associate the appearance of dice with being screwed over, they're going to react accordingly. They're absolutely justified in reacting accordingly if that's the case. The key is ensuring that's <i>not</i> the case.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's already an XP reward in the books for good role-play. I don't see any good for increasing the reward just for this circumstance, and there needs to be an incentive for more than one social roll per session. </div> </div> </div>Who said anything about increasing the reward? That circumstance is an example of good roleplay, and they get the reward for good roleplay.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In my house rules, subverting an order backed by a successful social roll (because it's against the characters interest and thus worth rolling in the first place), would cost WP, not reward it. At best it might neither reward or cost if the order causes a detrimental enough that the Soldier would take it as an illegal or unjust order. </div> </div> </div>That's changing the circumstances of the example I presented. To say an order is valid is to assume it is both legitimate and justified.<br /> <br /> For example, let's say a Technocrat's confidential informant Ally is suspected of knowing too much; that Technocrat is ordered to investigate the matter, and if necessary burn the asset by scrubbing their memory and breaking contact. The only thing about that order out of the ordinary is that it's pretty softball considering the potential solutions available to the Technocracy, indicating it may be a test of loyalty -- or simply a routine operational security sweep. In fact, this is a situation where a roll shouldn't be needed at all, unless the player wants to roll Perception to see if there's more to it.<br /> <br /> In any case, chances are strong the PC is going either lose that Ally, or put a whole lot more at risk if they try to protect that Ally where protection is unwarranted, which means at the end of the day the PC's interest is <i>not</i> being served. The only limit on the number of ways to take it from there is imagination. Maybe the investigation doesn't turn up anything, and the PC is commended for their prompt and effective work. Maybe the investigation does turn up something, but the CI is too valuable to simply burn. Maybe something turns up, and the PC faces some personal or moral dilemma about burning the asset. Maybe the CI's an RT, and that PC faces some major blowback for simply having associated with them.<br /> <br /> Where that character goes as directed by the player is up to that player, and should be in accordance with that character's Nature and their relationship with involved parties.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 10-26-2019, 11:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-26T12:52:13-07:00","id":1343836,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1343779#post1343779\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Once again, that's a matter of what level of influence is being applied on a per-case basis; this is why I made it clear it's important to consider the source of the influence, the means employed to effect it, and the totality of circumstances around the roll.</div> </div> </div> </div>All of this is included in my ideas without making it an ad hoc approach.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Not all social rolls <i>are</i> equal or exist in a vacuum, and a key part of this conversation is that not all social rolls are or should be considered &quot;my way or the highway&quot; affairs; </div> </div> </div>This statement makes it significantly feel like you're not really reading the totality of my posts and just cherry picking stuff to disagree with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> An intimidation check <i>shouldn't</i> have equal weight as an attempt to lie, persuade, or negotiate a deal -- different means of exerting influence should have different outcomes in the wake of a player decision to comply, subvert, or resist. </div> </div> </div>The inequality of weight here is in consequences and stakes, which are things beyond the success/failure and potential resisting of the attempt itself.<br /> <br /> Nothing I've suggested says that subverting an Intimidation attempt by a bouncer to get you to get lost and keep out of a club is going to have the same consequences as a CEO subverting a successful roll to make a deal. The WP costs might be the same, but there's a lot of other stuff going on too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In other words, this is not a situation appropriately handled within the sole, or even primary, purview of crunch. </div> </div> </div>Great.<br /> <br /> This is, however, a discussion about allowing social rules on PCs, to which I offered some house rules to help smooth that over since the RAW encourage players to guard jealously against such efforts instead of going with the rolls and story.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The situation of one or more players feeling like they can assert their way out of NPC social influence at all times and under any circumstances is indicative of a player or storyteller problem, not a game mechanics problem. </div> </div> </div>Yes, but &quot;indicative,&quot; isn't definitive. Actually diving into the RAW shows there's a mechanics problem too.<br /> <br /> In general, there's plenty of times when player/GM problems stem from system problems, because the social contract of the game and the rules of the game are at odds with each other.<br /> <br /> To go beyond social mechanics for a moment, there's a classic design/player feedback loop when it comes to single use items. Games very easily fall into a design trap of wanting players to value such items, but end up doing so in a result that incentivizes players to hoard such items even when it's completely illogical to do so. I have seen countless cRPGs where players have a giant stash of potions and what not...even though they just beat the game and were still hoarding them &quot;just in case,&quot; after beating the final boss. Meanwhile IME the Dark Souls games tend to encourage lots of energy into collecting such things, but because you can only hold a few and they replenish automatically fairly regularly, you end up using them all the time instead of holding on to them.<br /> <br /> Simply put, mechanics matter.<br /> <br /> Plenty of player/GM problems have at least partial roots in players responding to the incentives of the rules.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The point being, if the players associate the appearance of dice with being screwed over, they're going to react accordingly. They're absolutely justified in reacting accordingly if that's the case. The key is ensuring that's <i>not</i> the case. </div> </div> </div>Having better social mechanics is one way to ensure this isn't the case, and one that the game can add to the table rather than everything being put on the GM. There being mechanics to follow helps assure players that social situations + dice = fair outcomes, not getting screwed over, because they know there's a process that the GM isn't deciding ad hoc and by fiat.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Who said anything about increasing the reward? That circumstance is an example of good roleplay, and they get the reward for good roleplay. </div> </div> </div>Then see the rest of my post where I point out the need for repeat incentives in the loop. 1 XP at the end of a session, which you could get without it, doesn't work for this design goal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's changing the circumstances of the example I presented. </div> </div> </div>The first half doesn't change anything, you just got my statements backwards and aren't acknowledging it. The second half changed it to a worse scenario and how that still wouldn't result in what you said.<br /> <br /> Again, I'm not really feeling like you're reading my posts all that closely.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In fact, this is a situation where a roll shouldn't be needed at all, unless the player wants to roll Perception to see if there's more to it. </div> </div> </div>Agreed, which means it doesn't really serve much in the ways of examples.<br /> <br /> My ideas are not meant to override the, &quot;only roll when it it matters,&quot; general mentality of the game; nor have I asserted they should.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...which means at the end of the day the PC's interest is <i>not</i> being served. </div> </div> </div>So what? Seriously. Why does this matter to anything I'm suggesting?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Where that character goes as directed by the player is up to that player, and should be in accordance with that character's Nature and their relationship with involved parties. </div> </div> </div>Which is... why this example has nothing to do with applying social rolls to PCs... this isn't a case where a roll would be called for in the first place, and the rest is just talking about making a decide plot out of a hook.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-26T13:59:07-07:00","id":1343858,"title":"","content":"I do pretty much the same thing as Heavy Arms in my games. NPCs can make Social rolls against PCs, and if the NPCs win, I give the players a stick-and-carrot incentive to abide by the roll results: if they go along with whatever the NPC is trying to persuade them into, they get a small amount of XP, or they can spend Willpower to not go along with the NPCs. If the NPC's roll beats theirs by enough successes, not going along may cost extra Willpower. <br /> <br /> I also use these mechanics when two PCs are making Social rolls against one another. <br /> <br /> My players have been pretty happy with it. It's not mind control, so they're still free to do what they want, but there is incentive to do what the NPCs want. I've seen some players take the XP and I've seen others spend Willpower to stick by their guns. It's really depended on what the context is and what's at stake.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-26T16:17:35-07:00","id":1343880,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> I agree that scopolamine may not work like the TV or documentaries show it. But there is definitely drugs that makes people waaay more sucseptible to suggestions. My point is that I think that being drugged is a lot stronger than the modifiers allowed in the game.<br /> <br /> I'd give -3 on the roll for manipulation+subterfuge for example if the manipulator is a friend of the target and the suggestion is something plausible that the target would consider doing. For example, if one guy is trying to convince his best friend and recorrent criminal to do one last perfect crime ( \u00e0 La Oncean`s eleven, with Bradd Pitt and George Clooney).<br /> <br /> Now get one example where a PC (PC1) is told to not let anyone pass beyond a certain door. Then another PC (PC2) casts a spell to drug PC1 with some strong drug like scopolamine, or anesthesia, or anything like that. Hell, he's a mage, he could even invent a freacking drug that affects directly the target frontal lobe (part of the brain responsible for making choices and recognize consequences). Or even affect his level of oxytocin/dopamine/serotonin (happy hormones), or directly affecting his mood to make PC1 so happy that he forgets what he's supposed to do and take any suggestion as an excellent idea.<br /> <br /> Now, you're telling me that that level of influence on the body/brain would be the same as a friend asking a favor? My point is that -3 is not enough to represent a completely drugged/affected person.<br /> <br /> The WP expenditure idea is very cool: for regular rolls. A drugged person, and remember, that this is <b>not</b> a regular drug. It can be an effect acting way stronger and directly on the desired area (frontal lobe), something that modern medicine cannot do, should not be able to just spend one WP and go exactly as if sober.<br /> <br /> In my example of PC1 guarding a door and PC2 drugging him, PC2 is making 2 rolls. One Arete roll for drugging and one social roll. If the Arete roll is successfull, then PC1 is drugged and easily manipulated. If PC2 also wins the social roll, then PC1 could just go &quot;fuck it, I'm spending one WP point and I'm standing in front of that door like a british royal soldier&quot; ?<br /> I think this is not fair, afterall he's out of his mind. Could a drugged person spend WP in the first place?<br /> <br /> Also, what if PC2 just try again? Another WP must be spent, or you'd make it last for the scene and PC1 isn't going to do shit?<br /> <br /> PS: Also note that I'm using 2 PCs in my example, to take the responsibility of the ST on this. I want to know how to deal with this when 2 PCs colide.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-26T19:55:30-07:00","id":1343913,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1343836#post1343836\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This statement makes it significantly feel like you're not really reading the totality of my posts and just cherry picking stuff to disagree with.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not exactly the case. We've discussed crunch versus fluff enough for me to realize you're a crunch-heavy player and I'm not, and there are certain areas with which we simply will never see eye to eye. Ergo, I'm presenting an argument for managing social conflict through narrative as opposed to mechanics as a counter-position to your own. As I see it, either are valid depending on players' and ST's priorities and interests, and what is important is both positions are represented.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The inequality of weight here is in consequences and stakes...The WP costs might be the same, but there's a lot of other stuff going on too. </div> </div> </div>This is part of the issue -- which rolls, and the immediate impact thereof, are &quot;worth&quot; requiring players to spend Willpower to resist? The implication of this line of thought, is that player agency is suspended unless the player chooses to preserve it by Willpower expenditure, and that in itself requires a deeper examination of which social actions are capable of exerting that level of influence, and at the end of the day that's going to be a judgment call by the storyteller unless you do indeed treat all social actions as equal.<br /> <br /> When we're talking about social actions, that's a <i>huge</i> net to throw, one large enough large enough blanket rules cannot and should not apply. The OP's implication is clear -- actions of sufficient assertiveness and consequence they may lead to degradation or outright loss of player agency. The reality is that's a fairly narrow sliver of potential actions that may fall under the &quot;social&quot; category, and we need to be careful about how and why we discuss this lest invoking the spectre of unintended consequences or disproportionate costs and benefits.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Having better social mechanics is one way to ensure this isn't the case, and one that the game can add to the table rather than everything being put on the GM. There being mechanics to follow helps assure players that social situations + dice = fair outcomes, not getting screwed over, because they know there's a process that the GM isn't deciding ad hoc and by fiat. </div> </div> </div>We're talking past each other on this point. You're discussing what is to happen once dice are in hand; I'm calling for a deeper examination into the thought processes that lead players or storytellers to reach for the dice in the first place. More to the point, if the storyteller is only reaching for the dice in circumstances that lead to loss of player agency, players are going to figure that out and react accordingly. The fix is to attenuate circumstances such the loss of agency is not the only possible outcome of a dice roll in the ST's favor, so to speak; the impact of that roll should be in proportion to the action that called for it.<br /> <br /> In other words, if I call for a roll-off in the course of haggling between a PC and NPC, the result of the dice roll should determine that NPC's final offer. Final say as to whether that offer is accepted or refused should fall to the player, without consequence or incentive. That roll shouldn't have the same determinant payoff matrix as, say, an order to do something backed by a threat of physical violence.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My ideas are not meant to override the, &quot;only roll when it it matters,&quot; general mentality of the game; nor have I asserted they should....this isn't a case where a roll would be called for in the first place... </div> </div> </div>This is at loggerheads with this statement,<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...because it's against the characters interest and thus worth rolling in the first place... </div> </div> </div>and this strikes to the heart of the matter. Opposed rolls in this situation arise when character (player or non-player) interests are in conflict, and the intent of the roll is to determine whose interest prevails. You previously argued yourself this is exactly why a roll <i>should</i> be called for, but amended your statement when I pointed out this really <i>wouldn't</i> be a situation in which dice needed to get involved by default. The reality is neither one of us are necessarily right or wrong -- the circumstances behind that action are what determine whether a roll by any involved party would be necessary, namely the NPC's intent in this example. Something I suspect you clued into, by arguing if the orders in my example were so detrimental to the PC they may be interpreted as invalid.<br /> <br /> This is why &quot;one size fits all&quot; rules <i>shouldn't</i> apply. The storyteller needs to determine what degree of player agency is at stake based upon the totality of the circumstances behind any given roll, and adjudicate costs and benefits proportionate to that degree of lost agency. Because, as has just been demonstrated, even the act of reaching for the dice is <i>already</i> a judgment call.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-27T00:53:13-07:00","id":1343935,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1343880#post1343880\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree that scopolamine may not work like the TV or documentaries show it.</div> </div> </div> </div>The VICE piece on it isn't a documentary. It's a poorly researched bit of yellow journalism.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But there is definitely drugs that makes people waaay more sucseptible to suggestions. </div> </div> </div>Please name one that doesn't take weeks, if not months, of constant dosing <b>combined</b> with psychological manipulation (such as isolation tactics) to actually have a demonstrable effect on this.<br /> <br /> As it stands, there's no drug that can do this in short term actions. That's why there's no effective truth serum. That's why police interrogation tactics often result in false statements by witnesses/suspects, and why torture doesn't work as a method of gathering intelligence. However you try to influence brain chemistry, it doesn't create quick rapport, and in fact creates lots of false positives in results. You can circumvent the conscious mind temporarily to influence actions, but you're not actually convincing them of anything.<br /> <br /> In effect, drugging someone in a short term fashion to the point of significant cognitive impairment is ultimately no different that just drugging them unconscious. You're past social influence because you're not even talking to a mind to get it to do something. You're just taking advantage of a partially mobile body that might respond to some cues involuntarily.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My point is that I think that being drugged is a lot stronger than the modifiers allowed in the game. </div> </div> </div>Magically drugging someone applies the drug rules to them, not the Magic Enhancing Abilities rules, and can do more than -3 to your difficulty.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now, you're telling me that that level of influence on the body/brain would be the same as a friend asking a favor? </div> </div> </div>No, I'm saying at that point you're knocking them out, or at least getting them staggeringly intoxicated, and them stopping you is no longer an issue of social rolls.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A drugged person, and remember, that this is <b>not</b> a regular drug. </div> </div> </div>Unless you want to up the power of your magic, it's a regular drug. Creating magically powerful drugs requires higher magic (and Matter). Magically created stuff is regular stuff unless you tack on more magic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It can be an effect acting way stronger and directly on the desired area (frontal lobe), something that modern medicine cannot do, should not be able to just spend one WP and go exactly as if sober. </div> </div> </div>That's... not really how the brain works. But again, if you turn off someone's frontal lobe? You're no longer in social rolls any more. We're in mind-control areas.<br /> <br /> ------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1343913#post1343913\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's not exactly the case.</div> </div> </div> </div>My impression could be wrong, but it would help if you actually address the concern instead of what you've posted.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We've discussed crunch versus fluff enough for me to realize you're a crunch-heavy player and I'm not, and there are certain areas with which we simply will never see eye to eye. </div> </div> </div>Actually I'm a &quot;mechanics matter&quot; player. I enjoy playing from light to heavy. However, I want the mechanics to actually perform as the system is designed. The ST system isn't a light system that runs on purely narrative logic, so I approach it as it's designed. There are rules in the ST system for social situations, and I find them unsatisfactory at doing their job within the framework of the rest of the system. I believe that the current rules lead to a strong negative player reaction to having social mechanics turned at them, and thus I seek to resolve that within the ST system, rather than turning the system into something else.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ....and what is important is both positions are represented. </div> </div> </div>Not really. You're free to present your own opinions as you feel they're useful. Just doing them because of some idea that it's important to &quot;counter balance&quot; my ideas isn't anything but annoying. Especially because you're not really arguing for a design aesthetic of philosophy, you're arguing against without a true replacement. All the &quot;it needs to be ad hoc!&quot; could be applied to any rule in any RPG.<br /> <br /> We're not having a formal debate where we're each representing a pro or con on a subject, and having both sides is actually necessary.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is part of the issue -- which rolls, and the immediate impact thereof, are &quot;worth&quot; requiring players to spend Willpower to resist? </div> </div> </div>If it's important enough to be rolling in the first place, it's important enough to offer incentive.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that the RAW are already written this way. It's just on a binary of follow the results dictated by the rules, or spend a WP to ignore the result of the roll. All I'm doing is expanding the options of how to respond to a successful roll, and advocating a more holistic and preset set of modifiers to social rolls rather than set difficulties (even variable ones).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The implication of this line of thought, is that player agency is suspended unless the player chooses to preserve it by Willpower expenditure,... </div> </div> </div>No, it implies a shift in player agency from, &quot;does my character follow or ignore the social influence,&quot; to, &quot;how do I decide my character's reactions to the social influence.&quot;<br /> <br /> Which is why a deeper examination isn't actually necessary.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm calling for a deeper examination into the thought processes that lead players or storytellers to reach for the dice in the first place. </div> </div> </div>What does this have to do with the OP's question, or even my suggestions? Everything can be discussed under the assumption that the thought process to get to dice has already been gone through in a thoughtful fashion.<br /> <br /> I find it very weird that you're the only claiming the &quot;rules light&quot; to my &quot;crunch heavy&quot; preferences, while I'm calling for a lighter generic social mechanic, and what you're calling for is actually a more complex system that needs to get into multiple types of social actions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> More to the point, if the storyteller is only reaching for the dice in circumstances that lead to loss of player agency, players are going to figure that out and react accordingly. </div> </div> </div>This isn't the situation at hand. The problem is the mechanics don't leave the ST a choice if they bring the dice out in social actions against players. Social actions in the system focus on taking away agency instead of altering the way agency is expressed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is at loggerheads with this statement, </div> </div> </div>No, it really isn't. It's just more clear cherry picking.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Opposed rolls in this situation arise when character (player or non-player) interests are in conflict,,, </div> </div> </div>That might be how you house rule things, but it's not actually the RAW. In M20, a facedown is an opposed roll, while a fake-out isn't, despite both representing situations where interests can be in conflict, because the RAW use opposed actions when both characters are in active competition (a facedown is a two-sided dominance contest, a fake-out is a one-sided attempt at deception) rather than the stakes of the roll.<br /> <br /> Trying to fast-talk your way past a guard isn't an opposed roll even though it's in a guard's interest to not let people without permission past, because the guard isn't actively competing with the person trying to talk past them.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-27T09:14:33-07:00","id":1344000,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1343880#post1343880\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a>But there is definitely drugs that makes people waaay more sucseptible to suggestions.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1343935#post1343935\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Please name one that doesn't take weeks, if not months, of constant dosing</div> </div> </div> </div>Alcohol <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> I'm kidding, but only halfway in that it also requires a social setup and doesn't go against the Nature of a character. If anything it pushes it out to the front - which can be exactly what one wants to push suggestions past the Demeanor barrier.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 10-27-2019, 03:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-27T09:53:46-07:00","id":1344009,"title":"","content":"Since torture has been mentioned, here's my two cents.<br /> <br /> Regular torture, the kind that exists IRL, works very well if you need information that is falsifiable. For example, if you need to torture someone for the code to a safe, and they lie, then you get instant feedback on their lie. The longer it takes to falsify the information given to you the less effective torture is.<br /> <br /> So torture is not a good means of gathering information, in most cases. The tortured person believes they will be killed since torture radicalizes most victims - it tells them that every worst expectation they had of you is true. Therefore they are more likely to lash out with a &quot;fuck you&quot; response that is as inconvenient for you as possible, giving believable false intelligence.<br /> <br /> Here's a quick summary based on what I've read:<br /> <br /> - torture is effective when it comes to acquiring rapidly falsifiable information like a code or nearby location<br /> <br /> - torture is an effective means of intimidating a person and a populace in some cases - historically it has been used more often as a punishment in reality<br /> <br /> - torture can be effective in eliciting the exact coerced response you want from a person - if you want someone to admit they killed someone, torture can make them do it... and torture IS an effective means if you don't actually care whether they are guilty. A fake confession can be a great excuse for cracking down on a group within society that you're not fond of - after all one of them just admitted to conspiring to kill the President!<br /> <br /> - torture is mostly ineffective if the information you're seeking is time-sensitive - a captive of even average intelligence will realize that they only need to hold out a certain length of time, which will strengthen their resolve<br /> <br /> - torture ensures that your captive has their worst suspicions of their captor confirmed - when the tables are turned (by the captive's organization, allies or friends, if not themselves) they will use similar methods<br /> <br /> - torture is primarily ordered and supported by those who don't perform or witness it - because it has so little intelligence value and does so much damage to both the tortured and the torturer, and actually loses you potential informants and allies, it only continues since those who order it are detached from it<br /> <br /> Note that bargaining isn't the same as torture; &quot;if you give us this time-sensitive non-falsifiable information, and we find out it's true in due course, then you and your family will be protected etc&quot;. Torturers can utilize bargaining, and often it is the bargaining which is successful and not the torture.<br /> <br /> MAGE STUFF<br /> <br /> Now since Mage is, well, Mage, using torture to perform a Mind 2 to 4 effect where you trawl through a person's mind is completely valid. But it's a method which has loads of downsides, and there are many other options. So Mages would only perform torture if they BELIEVED it to be a highly effective means of extracting intelligence (and this myth is prevalent throughout society, and within some law enforcement and intelligence communities), or if they LIKED it as a means of gaining information (this would apply to Nephandi, sociopathic mages, mages who practice darker or sadistic magick).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 10-27-2019, 12:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-27T10:37:40-07:00","id":1344016,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1343935#post1343935\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Please name one that doesn't take weeks, if not months, of constant dosing <b>combined</b> with psychological manipulation (such as isolation tactics) to actually have a demonstrable effect on this.<br /> <br /> As it stands, there's no drug that can do this in short term actions. That's why there's no effective truth serum. That's why police interrogation tactics often result in false statements by witnesses/suspects, and why torture doesn't work as a method of gathering intelligence. However you try to influence brain chemistry, it doesn't create quick rapport, and in fact creates lots of false positives in results. You can circumvent the conscious mind temporarily to influence actions, but you're not actually convincing them of anything.<br /> <br /> In effect, drugging someone in a short term fashion to the point of significant cognitive impairment is ultimately no different that just drugging them unconscious. You're past social influence because you're not even talking to a mind to get it to do something. You're just taking advantage of a partially mobile body that might respond to some cues involuntarily.</div> </div> </div> </div>Dude, you've never been drunk and way more susceptible to, often stupid, suggestions? Scopolamine is a rare drug that we have no good data about, but people on the effect of anesthesia is very easily manipulated and believe in any lie.<br /> <br /> To be fair, I have no experience on illegal drugs and their exact effects. But I think you're thinking too hard about this. Storyteller often have hollywoodian ttake on things, like explosions, combat, etc. ST is not a very realistic set and having a magickally created drug to affect a person common sense is not something too out of the game.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Magically drugging someone applies the drug rules to them, not the Magic Enhancing Abilities rules, and can do more than -3 to your difficulty. </div> </div> </div>You could rule this way, yes. But there is no much benefit in going beyond -3, since diff is normally 6 and even less if WP expenditure is allowed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No, I'm saying at that point you're knocking them out, or at least getting them staggeringly intoxicated, and them stopping you is no longer an issue of social rolls. </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't say knocked out, but definitely intoxicated. While this wouldn't work to get the truth out of someone (don't even know why you brought that), it definitely should to get someone to do something you want them to do.<br /> If you don't think social rolls apply, then what?<br /> <br /> [/quote]<br /> That's... not really how the brain works. But again, if you turn off someone's frontal lobe? You're no longer in social rolls any more. We're in mind-control areas[/QUOTE]<br /> <br /> Hdydt has the &quot;tweaking chemistry&quot; thing to use Life to affect people's behavior. Mind can also affect the mood of someone. A very, and I mean very, gappy person is way more prone to suggestions than a grumpy person. The mage can also tweak his own pheromones to make it nore efficient.<br /> <br /> Blw, you would also argue that that's noy how pheromones work, and I'd agree. But Mage isn't a realistic scenario and pheromones do affect people like this. I'm not turning the frontal lobe off, just affecting it the way some drugs do. <br /> <br /> So, I ask again, how would you rule someone directly affecting a target brain. Use my PC1 and PC2 example, please.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-27T14:30:23-07:00","id":1344069,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344009#post1344009\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Regular torture, the kind that exists IRL, works very well if you need information that is falsifiable.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is that it's good for getting an answer, not necessarily for getting the true answer; as I noted, it results in a lot of false positives. It's pretty much impossible to tell the difference between being lied to because the subject doesn't actually know the true answer but is willing to lie in hopes that you'll stop, or if they're lying because they believe that there's no upside to telling you the truth.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - torture is an effective means of intimidating a person and a populace in some cases - historically it has been used more often as a punishment in reality<br /> ...<br /> - torture can be effective in eliciting the exact coerced response you want from a person - if you want someone to admit they killed someone, torture can make them do it... and torture IS an effective means if you don't actually care whether they are guilty. </div> </div> </div>Which is... sorta beyond the point of my reference to torture to get information out of people.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344016#post1344016\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Dude, you've never been drunk and way more susceptible to, often stupid, suggestions?</div> </div> </div> </div>There's an important distinction here. Alcohol lowers inhibitions, rather than making you more suggestible. Lowering inhibitions means whatever people are pressuring you to do, you need to want to do them in the first place. You want to do wild and crazy stunts to show off, but most of the time you let the sound logic of that being stupid and dangerous inhibit your actions. Then you get drunk and you worry less about the consequences, and more about the desires you already had.<br /> <br /> Suggestibility is a metric about getting people to do something they were not already inclined to do (not necessarily opposed, but not desiring either). Alcohol is a very weak drug for such efforts (even the long term dosing option, because humans tend to build tolerances to alcohol fairly quickly).<br /> <br /> This is where fast acting drugs fall down, and anything beyond a small modifier is pointless as you get into heavy intoxication instead.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Scopolamine is a rare drug that we have no good data about,... </div> </div> </div>Where on Earth are you getting this from? Scopolamine has been in use in modern medicine for almost 140 years, and in traditional medicine for thousands. It's well known, well studied, and is only rare in the sense that it's rarely used anymore because we have better medications for most applications (many of which are synthetic derivatives of it). It's produced by a large number of plants within the nightshade family, and thanks to ornamental use can be found on every habitable continent.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ....but people on the effect of anesthesia is very easily manipulated and believe in any lie. </div> </div> </div>...huh?<br /> <br /> First off, anesthesia is a medical state, not a drug.<br /> <br /> Second, being under the effects of general anesthesia doesn't do that... because the patient is unconscious. The whole point of local anesthetic applications is that they don't impact anything besides the treatment site, and thus wouldn't impact the brain. If you sedate someone to the point of semi-lucidity where they'll respond to communication, they're not actually having a conversation with you... their brain is just going through the motions. The odds of them even remembering a conversation even happened are tiny, and actually remember the topic practically non-existent. Since anesthesia blocks memory formation, you can't implant lies people will wake up believing are true later... because they can't form memories of what you said.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But I think you're thinking too hard about this. </div> </div> </div>No, I happen to know a good deal about this for other reasons, and I'm pushing back against things being stated as facts that aren't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Storyteller often have hollywoodian ttake on things, like explosions, combat, etc. ST is not a very realistic set and having a magickally created drug to affect a person common sense is not something too out of the game. </div> </div> </div>As I said before, this is what the Mythic Threads and Blatancy rules are for. If you want your mage to drug people and use magic to make the drug do far more than it's really capable of based on urban legends about what it's supposedly capable of? Mages should definitely be taking advantage of that to make things coincidental.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You could rule this way, yes. But there is no much benefit in going beyond -3, since diff is normally 6 and even less if WP expenditure is allowed. </div> </div> </div>Have you read the drug rules in M20? Stacking a -3 diff on your rolls, plus things like hitting the target with -3 dice on all their mundane rolls while having a major acid trip has plenty of benefit to it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...it definitely should to get someone to do something you want them to do. </div> </div> </div>Why? Under what biochemical mechanism?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you don't think social rolls apply, then what? </div> </div> </div>No most likely. If you're magically drugging someone so hard that the Magic Enhancing Abilities rules isn't sufficient, you're drugging them hard enough that (a) they no longer represent a meaningful obstacle, and/or (b) any social actions against them isn't actually getting through to their conscious brain and memory so it is, again, at best cuing involuntary responses anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hdydt has the &quot;tweaking chemistry&quot; thing to use Life to affect people's behavior. Mind can also affect the mood of someone. A very, and I mean very, gappy person is way more prone to suggestions than a grumpy person. The mage can also tweak his own pheromones to make it nore efficient. </div> </div> </div>And HDYDT directly says to apply the Magic Enhancing Abilities rules.... so I don't see the issue here. If you want to tweak someone's biochemistry... you can modify difficulties up to 3 steps. Otherwise you need to do more than tweak.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, I ask again, how would you rule someone directly affecting a target brain. Use my PC1 and PC2 example, please. </div> </div> </div>PC1 is rendered staggering intoxicated, and has to spend 1 WP just to act normally for a round unless it's specified that the magical effect is potent enough that this standard use of WP doesn't apply. PC2 can just avoid direct confrontation and get past PC1 being a guard, or PC2 can just stall PC1 until PC1 runs out of WP, and then go past them.<br /> <br /> Remember though, that in my suggestion if PC1 spends 1 WP to resist the impact of the drug, and 1 WP to subvert PC2's social roll (1 per roll... so this is burning a lot of WP) they can't just ignore the social roll, they have to engage with it. So if the situation gets forced into rolling:<br /> <br /> PC2 says to PC1, &quot;I'm allowed in, here's my ID.&quot; Social roll for the misdirection as the ID doesn't actually justify them getting in, but PC1 is so wasted they'd miss that.<br /> PC1's player spend 2 WP to ignore the drug and to subvert the roll, says, &quot;I don't recognize your name from the list, let me check with my supervisor.&quot;<br /> PC2 says, &quot;Hey, you don't need to call in, you've got the list right there.&quot; And points to a clipboard, crumbling up paper, or whatever would be good enough for it to be the list to the addled mind, and another roll.&quot;<br /> PC1's player spends 2 more WP to ignore the drug and subvert the roll, &quot;Wow, how'd I miss that, thanks, I'll look.... um... I don't see you here though...&quot;<br /> PC2, &quot;Here I am, right there, see?&quot;<br /> <br /> And eventually PC1's player either cuts their loses and stops bleeding WP like crazy, or PC1 runs out of WP, and PC2 gets past them.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-27T15:39:22-07:00","id":1344081,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344069#post1344069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem is that it's good for getting an answer, not necessarily for getting the true answer; as I noted, it results in a lot of false positives. It's pretty much impossible to tell the difference between being lied to because the subject doesn't actually know the true answer but is willing to lie in hopes that you'll stop, or if they're lying because they believe that there's no upside to telling you the truth.<br /> <br /> Which is... sorta beyond the point of my reference to torture to get information out of people.</div> </div> </div> </div>- that's why I'm talking about falsifiable intelligence - if they've made up a combination for the safe, or are lying to say &quot;fuck you&quot;, you can try the combination right in front of them and escalate the torture as punishment for lying. It does create an incentive to tell the truth - once I tell them, even if they kill me, they won't keep torturing me. So in the niche situation of needing falsifiable intelligence that your victim could reasonably know, torture *can* work.<br /> <br /> - I brought up the punishment/coercion/dehumanization angles because it's relevant, even if it isn't about information or intelligence - if torture is almost useless for gathering intel it does make you wonder why it's used, and it having other uses is relevant. And sometimes that's relevant at the tabletop, hence why I shared that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-27T16:41:25-07:00","id":1344087,"title":"","content":"The immediately falsifiable aspect doesn't really change the counter-arguments against the effectiveness of torture. Just because you can show immediately that an answer is false doesn't tell you why you got a false answer. Did they lie about knowing because you were torturing them? Did they try to give you an honest answer but remembered things incorrectly (the code was 12345, but they said 12354)? Or are they actually just lying to you despite knowing the truth? All you know is that the answer was false, not why, and thus don't know how best to proceed. Torturing someone that doesn't actually know the answer is futile in the first place even if there was a high likelihood they'd know based on other intel. Increasing torture on someone that though they were honestly giving in but made a mistake runs a risk of shutting them down. If they're purposefully lying after torture, there's no reason to believe more pain will change whatever gave them the mentality to lie directly in the first place.<br /> <br /> And lets be realistic here, the whole premise of an immediately falsifiable piece of information, that's no under a time demand (or related to time sensitivity, something like a passcode might have an autolock after too many failed incorrect attempts), and has no non-torture solutions that are at least as effective and definitely more moral, quickly become rather fanciful to meet all these criteria.<br /> <br /> There's no such thing as an unbreakable safe. Why spend days torturing someone when you could just call a professional safe cracker (assuming you don't have one as a group with the resources to hold people captive and torture them without intervention or oversight)? Enemy troop deployments/etc. are going to become time sensitive the moment the enemy realizes you've captured someone that has the information to spill even if it wasn't immediately time sensitive before. Oh, and as many intelligence operatives have testified to, the adage of catching more flies with honey than vinegar applies to this as well when it comes to effectiveness. Etc., etc., etc. <br /> <br /> Taking everything into account, even situations where torture might yield useful information, there's no defense of it. It could work, but there's always a better option in anything but the most contrived hypothetical examples, and that's before factoring in the moral/ethical side of it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-27T17:39:00-07:00","id":1344100,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344069#post1344069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's an important distinction here. Alcohol lowers inhibitions, rather than making you more suggestible. Lowering inhibitions means whatever people are pressuring you to do, you need to want to do them in the first place. You want to do wild and crazy stunts to show off, but most of the time you let the sound logic of that being stupid and dangerous inhibit your actions. Then you get drunk and you worry less about the consequences, and more about the desires you already had.<br /> <br /> Suggestibility is a metric about getting people to do something they were not already inclined to do (not necessarily opposed, but not desiring either). Alcohol is a very weak drug for such efforts (even the long term dosing option, because humans tend to build tolerances to alcohol fairly quickly).<br /> <br /> This is where fast acting drugs fall down, and anything beyond a small modifier is pointless as you get into heavy intoxication instead.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough. Alcohol was just an example, since it is the most common mind affecting &quot;drug&quot;, at least where I live.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344069#post1344069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Where on Earth are you getting this from? Scopolamine has been in use in modern medicine for almost 140 years, and in traditional medicine for thousands. It's well known, well studied, and is only rare in the sense that it's rarely used anymore because we have better medications for most applications (many of which are synthetic derivatives of it). It's produced by a large number of plants within the nightshade family, and thanks to ornamental use can be found on every habitable continent.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I'm getting this from a documentary that I saw. Also, I am talking about the use of scopolamine the &quot;devil breath&quot; as a drug, not in a medical way. Modern medicine might use it together with other substances and in an ammount that will not &quot;harm&quot;the brain. But I admit that I didn't delve enough about this, was just curious.<br /> And since my point isn't to bring real life drugs with real life effects into game, maybe we should not discuss this further. What is important for us to agree is that there are drugs that affect the brain's capacity of using common sense, make choices, and remembering. That should be enough for the game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344069#post1344069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...huh?<br /> <br /> First off, anesthesia is a medical state, not a drug.<br /> <br /> Second, being under the effects of general anesthesia doesn't do that... because the patient is unconscious. The whole point of local anesthetic applications is that they don't impact anything besides the treatment site, and thus wouldn't impact the brain. If you sedate someone to the point of semi-lucidity where they'll respond to communication, they're not actually having a conversation with you... their brain is just going through the motions. The odds of them even remembering a conversation even happened are tiny, and actually remember the topic practically non-existent. Since anesthesia blocks memory formation, you can't implant lies people will wake up believing are true later... because they can't form memories of what you said.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I am not of the health area, so I don't know details about anesthesias. What I mean is that when a person wake up from anesthesia (or is about to go down, can't remember) that person begins talking nonsense and agreeing with everything. Also, you're right that they often don't remember most of the conversations, which is only better for the mage.<br /> I'm saying this from personal experience since I was once under sedation before a surgery and I suffered those effects. A friend of mine also gone through that. You can see videos on YouTube of people under sedation that can't stop laughing, etc.<br /> While this is not a good result for getting the truth out of someone, it is perfect for manipulating them into doing something, like passing through a guard or getting the bartender to borrow you his shotgun just for a sec.<br /> <br /> For info gathering, there are other effects (like a fear drug) that could work with intimidation. But I agree that this falls under the issues that you are arguing on the torture thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344069#post1344069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, I happen to know a good deal about this for other reasons, and I'm pushing back against things being stated as facts that aren't.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> As I said before, this is what the Mythic Threads and Blatancy rules are for. If you want your mage to drug people and use magic to make the drug do far more than it's really capable of based on urban legends about what it's supposedly capable of? Mages should definitely be taking advantage of that to make things coincidental.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Have you read the drug rules in M20? Stacking a -3 diff on your rolls, plus things like hitting the target with -3 dice on all their mundane rolls while having a major acid trip has plenty of benefit to it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Gonna read that section again.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344069#post1344069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why? Under what biochemical mechanism?</div> </div> </div> </div>The mechanism of affecting the part of the brain that deals with decision making. You don't have to be medical expert specific to cast an effect. You don't have to be a physic and calculate all the equations to cast a Forces effect. It just has to be pausible.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344069#post1344069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No most likely. If you're magically drugging someone so hard that the Magic Enhancing Abilities rules isn't sufficient, you're drugging them hard enough that (a) they no longer represent a meaningful obstacle, and/or (b) any social actions against them isn't actually getting through to their conscious brain and memory so it is, again, at best cuing involuntary responses anyway.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> And HDYDT directly says to apply the Magic Enhancing Abilities rules.... so I don't see the issue here. If you want to tweak someone's biochemistry... you can modify difficulties up to 3 steps. Otherwise you need to do more than tweak.</div> </div> </div> </div>Perfect! No longer being an obstacle might be exactly what the mage wants. But how would you rule someone drugged in this level then? In my example of PC1 and PC2? <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344069#post1344069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">PC1 is rendered staggering intoxicated, and has to spend 1 WP just to act normally for a round unless it's specified that the magical effect is potent enough that this standard use of WP doesn't apply. PC2 can just avoid direct confrontation and get past PC1 being a guard, or PC2 can just stall PC1 until PC1 runs out of WP, and then go past them.<br /> <br /> Remember though, that in my suggestion if PC1 spends 1 WP to resist the impact of the drug, and 1 WP to subvert PC2's social roll (1 per roll... so this is burning a lot of WP) they can't just ignore the social roll, they have to engage with it. So if the situation gets forced into rolling:<br /> <br /> PC2 says to PC1, &quot;I'm allowed in, here's my ID.&quot; Social roll for the misdirection as the ID doesn't actually justify them getting in, but PC1 is so wasted they'd miss that.<br /> PC1's player spend 2 WP to ignore the drug and to subvert the roll, says, &quot;I don't recognize your name from the list, let me check with my supervisor.&quot;<br /> PC2 says, &quot;Hey, you don't need to call in, you've got the list right there.&quot; And points to a clipboard, crumbling up paper, or whatever would be good enough for it to be the list to the addled mind, and another roll.&quot;<br /> PC1's player spends 2 more WP to ignore the drug and subvert the roll, &quot;Wow, how'd I miss that, thanks, I'll look.... um... I don't see you here though...&quot;<br /> PC2, &quot;Here I am, right there, see?&quot;<br /> <br /> And eventually PC1's player either cuts their loses and stops bleeding WP like crazy, or PC1 runs out of WP, and PC2 gets past them.</div> </div> </div> </div>That fits nicely too. So, it's 2 WP per roll. Isn't there a limit for WP spent in a scene?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-27T18:49:36-07:00","id":1344109,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344100#post1344100\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, I'm getting this from a documentary that I saw. Also, I am talking about the use of scopolamine the &quot;devil breath&quot; as a drug, not in a medical way. Modern medicine might use it together with other substances and in an ammount that will not &quot;harm&quot;the brain.</div> </div> </div> </div>If it's the VICE piece (&quot;World's Scariest Drug: Colombian Devil's Breath&quot;), it's been debunked repeatedly by doctors.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What I mean is that when a person wake up from anesthesia (or is about to go down, can't remember) that person begins talking nonsense and agreeing with everything. </div> </div> </div>Yes, this is a common occurrence, it's just not &quot;real&quot; agreement. If I ask someone in that state to give me a million dollars and they agree, I didn't talk them into giving me all their money, I took advantage of their mental state to get them to say yes without meaning to agree to what I asked. It's a more complicated way of saying, &quot;saywhatifyou'regoingtogivemefivedollars,&quot; really fast to prompt people to say what... and most people aren't going to hand you any cash because of it. It's just a trick, not actually influencing people.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The mechanism of affecting the part of the brain that deals with decision making. You don't have to be medical expert specific to cast an effect. You don't have to be a physic and calculate all the equations to cast a Forces effect. It just has to be pausible. </div> </div> </div>Turning off the brain's ability to make decisions, and then asking that brain to make a decision to agree to your social influence isn't plausible. That's my issue with all this. You can't turn off the parts of the brain necessary to parse, &quot;Lend me your car, and I'll fill up the gas and give you some cash when I get back,&quot; and respond... and then try to get someone to agree to that deal. You might be able to manipulate them into saying the word &quot;yes&quot; but they're not actually agreeing because you turned off the part of their brain needed to agree.<br /> <br /> If I use Life magic to paralyze your legs... and then use Mind magic to command you to walk? You can't walk, so the Mind magic doesn't really do anything besides possibly torment you with an impossible command. That's basically what you're talking about doing, just with magically drugging people and social influence.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Isn't there a limit for WP spent in a scene? </div> </div> </div>How much WP you have.<br /> <br /> There's a limit on how many WP you can spend per turn to get extra successes (once), but otherwise there's no limit as long as you have the points to spend.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-28T08:28:32-07:00","id":1344195,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344109#post1344109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If it's the VICE piece (&amp;quot;World's Scariest Drug: Colombian Devil's Breath&amp;quot<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />, it's been debunked repeatedly by doctors.</div> </div> </div> </div>I honestly don't remember. But there is lots of references of it on TV. And that's enough to pull an effect based on it. Remember that the Life/Mind mage isn't necessarily going to literally put scopolamine into the target, just affect the brain similarly to those drugs.<br /> <br /> Again, the mage could tweak the effect. Like he could make someone drunk, but remove the target hangover in the next morning. That's why I say you shouldn't be so rigid in what the drug can or cannot do. This is just a example of what the effect could do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344109#post1344109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, this is a common occurrence, it's just not &amp;quot;real&amp;quot; agreement. If I ask someone in that state to give me a million dollars and they agree, I didn't talk them into giving me all their money, I took advantage of their mental state to get them to say yes without meaning to agree to what I asked. It's a more complicated way of saying, &amp;quot;saywhatifyou'regoingtogivemefivedollars,&amp;quo t; really fast to prompt people to say what... and most people aren't going to hand you any cash because of it. It's just a trick, not actually influencing people.</div> </div> </div> </div>I partially agree. While I agree that the person isn't really agreeing, if the mage insist on getting to a ATM snd getting money, there is a good chance the drugged person will do it.<br /> Once the effect runs off, the target won't remember and won't do anything that was asked. So, for long terme orders, like go spy on someone, this wouldn't work. But quick tasks can be made.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344109#post1344109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Turning off the brain's ability to make decisions, and then asking that brain to make a decision to agree to your social influence isn't plausible. That's my issue with all this. You can't turn off the parts of the brain necessary to parse, &amp;quot;Lend me your car, and I'll fill up the gas and give you some cash when I get back,&amp;quot; and respond... and then try to get someone to agree to that deal. You might be able to manipulate them into saying the word &amp;quot;yes&amp;quot; but they're not actually agreeing because you turned off the part of their brain needed to agree.<br /> <br /> If I use Life magic to paralyze your legs... and then use Mind magic to command you to walk? You can't walk, so the Mind magic doesn't really do anything besides possibly torment you with an impossible command. That's basically what you're talking about doing, just with magically drugging people and social influence.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is not turning off the brain capacity to make choices, it's just messing with it. Confusing it. Like shuffling the synapses ln that region, I don't know.<br /> The idea is not to mske him unable to mske a decision, but to discern a good one from a bad one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344109#post1344109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How much WP you have.<br /> <br /> There's a limit on how many WP you can spend per turn to get extra successes (once), but otherwise there's no limit as long as you have the points to spend.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm. I see. And what consequences would have a person that spends so much WP like this? Reaching 0 WP?<br /> And what if the target succeed in one roll? The mage should be able to keep trying manipulating..<br /> <br /> Also, your answer is ok for a Pc (PC2) affecting a NPC (PC2). But in my example, there are 2 PC. So you can't, as a ST, make the PC1 respond that way. Probably a PC won't answer this way, after spending the first WP he will try to avoid getting manipulated again, like attacking the mage (PC2). How would you deal with this?<br /> <br /> PS: Just read the drugs and poisons section again and I see that there are lots of substances that act by reducing dices. Also, I see no option to spend WP to ignore drugs effects.<br /> While WP can be spent to stop the urge of doing something by impulse, I don't think drugs effects is one of them. This way, the target should continue drugged. And now my issue with your system: should a highly drugged person be able to spenf WP to stop being manipulated?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-28T10:43:29-07:00","id":1344224,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344195#post1344195\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Remember that the Life/Mind mage isn't necessarily going to literally put scopolamine into the target, just affect the brain similarly to those drugs.</div> </div> </div> </div>Keeping in mind that many of these sorts of drugs work because they have effects on the whole body, not just the brain.<br /> <br /> Just mucking around with neurotransmitters and not having any somatic inputs from outside the CNS is going to alter someone's mood... but be exceedingly unpredictable. You actually want to literally tweak the full body chemistry of the subject so the brain doesn't freak out when it's doing stuff for no reason.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's why I say you shouldn't be so rigid in what the drug can or cannot do. This is just a example of what the effect could do. </div> </div> </div>The of the rigidity is the line between what's tweaking biochemistry, and what's more overt mind-control, and thus how to adjudicate what rules to use.<br /> <br /> If a mage is copying the effects of real drugs on the human body to manipulate people, they need to follow what drugs can actually do, and go from there.<br /> <br /> If a mage is using drugs as an Instrument, then they need to follow the magic rules on what Spheres are needed to accomplish the results they're after.<br /> <br /> I don't see any benefit towards blurring the line between the two, which is what it feels like you're trying to do in all this. Once we get into &quot;well they're not real drugs, so I can keep pushing up on the kind of impact they can have on people,&quot; it's not about how magically delivered chemical interactions should impact social influence, it's what Spheres you need to do what you're trying to accomplish that drugs alone can't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> While I agree that the person isn't really agreeing, if the mage insist on getting to a ATM snd getting money, there is a good chance the drugged person will do it. </div> </div> </div>Not really. I mean, here's the basic scenarios you're going to end up with:<br /> <br /> 1) Mage is escorting an obviously intoxicated person around, constantly telling them what to do, until they get to an ATM, and then the individual is going to fumble and bumble about as their motor control is shot to hell, maybe leaving the mage to take over and type things in for the subject, before walking away with some cash. Congrats, there are a lot of witnesses to your obvious criminal activity, including a high probability of it being caught on video by multiple cameras on different systems. Sure you might get some cash, but you're now a wanted criminal, yay.<br /> <br /> 2) The mage sends the subject off on their own, and any of thousands of things happen that stop it from working: Just bumping into someone could completely distract them from what the mage asked them to do and forget about going to the ATM as they just stumble off confused as to where they are and what they're doing. They have no memory system. The second, &quot;I need to go to the ATM&quot; is out of their thoughts, it's gone.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But quick tasks can be made. </div> </div> </div>Quick as in a few seconds, not a few minutes, and with severe motor control issues complicating any physical task; even pressing buttons or filling out a check.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It is not turning off the brain capacity to make choices, it's just messing with it. Confusing it. Like shuffling the synapses ln that region, I don't know.<br /> The idea is not to mske him unable to mske a decision, but to discern a good one from a bad one. </div> </div> </div>There isn't really a fundamental distinction to be had here. <br /> <br /> First, complex thoughts are not processed in one part of the brain, even if we know specific parts of the brain handle different things. The frontal lobe doesn't know &quot;good&quot; and &quot;bad.&quot; Those sorts of value judgements are handled elsewhere. The frontal lobe is what reasons through the options with inputs from the values having part of the brain and more. The frontal lobe, effectively, doesn't really &quot;know&quot; anything but what the rest of the brain is loudly shouting at it. It's job is to listen to all the loud shouting and figure out what the actual response should be. Turn it off, or confuse it, or whatever... you just get a jumble of inputs and no way to figure out what to do.<br /> <br /> Second.... just freaking Mind magic them already. If we're at the point of trying to temporarily rewire the brain to flip around things like value judgements... we're at the point of just mind controlling people. At what point do you let go of the attempt to try to justify this all under &quot;tweaking&quot; which by the books isn't supposed to go past difficulty modifiers anyway, and accept we're in the &quot;control&quot; side of all this?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hmm. I see. And what consequences would have a person that spends so much WP like this? Reaching 0 WP? </div> </div> </div>Yes. The game has no distinct downsides to running out of WP besides running out of WP and not being able to spend any until you get more back. Theoretically there's an RP downside as your character should be feeling the lack of WP and strong desire to go refill it, but it's weakly stated as a consideration at best.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And what if the target succeed in one roll? The mage should be able to keep trying manipulating.. </div> </div> </div>That assumes the target is even rolling, but if they win a roll the manipulator can keep trying, but that success of the target needs to play out too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Probably a PC won't answer this way, after spending the first WP he will try to avoid getting manipulated again, like attacking the mage (PC2). How would you deal with this? </div> </div> </div>Tell the player that attacking isn't a valid subversion of a successful roll. They have to engage with the successful roll and play off it, and can't just ignore it. It doesn't have to be &quot;call my supervisor,&quot; but it needs to be something along those lines to engage with the fact that they've been influenced into thinking that they've been presented with a valid ID and respond accordingly. The WP is to let them twist it so they can act incredulously and try to seek an external reason to counter the manipulation.<br /> <br /> &quot;You see a guard that's acting a bit intoxicated, someone walks up to the guard, the guard stops them, they show ID, and the guard acknowledges the ID is valid... and then starts attacking.&quot; It's a obviously not a good faith engagement when you narrate it out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> PS: Just read the drugs and poisons section again and I see that there are lots of substances that act by reducing dices. Also, I see no option to spend WP to ignore drugs effects. </div> </div> </div>Most drugs operate by dealing damage to determine their penalty, which you ignore by spending WP to ignore damage based penalties for one turn. Again though, the point here is a few seconds of concentration.... not just shrugging off the drug with a single WP expenditure.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> should a highly drugged person be able to spenf WP to stop being manipulated? </div> </div> </div>My system doesn't allow anyone to spend WP to stop being manipulated. They can spend WP to subvert being manipulated.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-28T20:05:31-07:00","id":1344332,"title":"","content":"Ok, at this point I cannot argue about the working of the brain. And as I said, neither should we. The game is not that deep into biology or physics anyway.<br /> <br /> About the game mechanics. I understand then, that you see no difference between a player trying to manipulate another player with or without the drugging thing besides the social rolls modifiers to the drugging scenario? Regarding how well the manipulated player should obey the suggestion and if he can or cannot use WP to stop it.<br /> <br /> In my player vs player scenario ( the guard is a player), the only difference of the target being drugged is indeed the modifiers?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-28T22:15:14-07:00","id":1344349,"title":"","content":"As I've said, it depends on the magic in question.<br /> <br /> If you're &quot;tweaking&quot; than yes, it's just modifiers. If you're stepping up to &quot;controlling&quot; than it's more than modifiers, but it's also getting away from social rolls being needed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-29T10:51:04-07:00","id":1344450,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344349#post1344349\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As I've said, it depends on the magic in question.<br /> <br /> If you're &quot;tweaking&quot; than yes, it's just modifiers. If you're stepping up to &quot;controlling&quot; than it's more than modifiers, but it's also getting away from social rolls being needed.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, using drugs would also force the target to spend 1 extra wp per roll for ignoring th drug effect.<br /> So, in your rules, the target will have his chances of resisting halved, and his wp drained a lot faster. <br /> <br /> It's a good rule, except for the wp ignoring drug effects thing. In this rule, someone who uses coicaine/speed to get extra actions could spend wp to ignore the negative effects? This work for all drugs that has positive effects","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-29T12:56:24-07:00","id":1344481,"title":"","content":"I would personally rule that if you're spending WP to ignore the effects of drugs temporarily, it would ignore all of them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-29T13:16:35-07:00","id":1344484,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1343519-social-rolls-against-pcs?p=1344481#post1344481\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would personally rule that if you're spending WP to ignore the effects of drugs temporarily, it would ignore all of them.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a hell of a Willpower point. Being just for 1 turn looks not so bad, though. <br /> <br /> I'd like to use this thread to ask for guidance refering the social rolls table. Maybe because I'm not native english speaker, but i cant figure out the differences of some rolls. For instance, what's the difference between fake-out and deception?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}